Implementing a multi-tree list (also known as a hierarchical tree view) in an Angular application involves several steps. Below is a basic implementation guide:

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project

First, make sure you have Node.js and npm installed on your machine.

Create a new Angular project:
```bash
ng new my-app
cd my-app
```

Install the necessary dependencies for managing JSON data:
```bash
npm install @types/node --save-dev
npm install json-server --save-dev
```

Run `json-server` to start a local server that will serve our data:
```bash
npx json-server --watch db.json --port 3001
```

### Step 2: Create the Tree Data

Create a file named `data.ts` in the `src/app/` directory to store your tree data:
```typescript
import { TreeNode } from './tree-node';

export class Data {
    public root: TreeNode = new TreeNode('Root', []);
}
```

Define the `TreeNode` interface:
```typescript
export interface TreeNode {
    name: string;
    children?: TreeNode[];
}
```

Create the initial JSON data structure:
```json
{
    "root": {
        "name": "Root",
        "children": [
            {
                "name": "Child 1"
            },
            {
                "name": "Child 2",
                "children": [
                    {
                        "name": "Grandchild 1"
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Implement the Multi-Tree List Component

Create a component to display the tree:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { Data } from '../data';
import * as fs from 'fs';
import * as path from 'path';

@Component({
    selector: 'app-multi-tree-list',
    templateUrl: './multi-tree-list.component.html',
    styleUrls: ['./multi-tree-list.component.css']
})
export class MultiTreeListComponent {
    private _data: Data;

    constructor() {
        this._data = new Data();
        this.loadData();
    }

    private loadData(): void {
        const jsonDataPath = path.join(__dirname, '../../db.json');
        if (!fs.existsSync(jsonDataPath)) return; // Handle missing JSON file case

        try {
            const jsonDataString = fs.readFileSync(jsonDataPath, 'utf8');
            const jsonData = JSON.parse(jsonDataString);
            this._data.root = new TreeNode(jsonData.root.name, jsonData.root.children.map(child => new TreeNode(child.name, child.children)));
        } catch (error) {
            console.error('Error loading data:', error);
        }
    }

    get data(): Data {
        return this._data;
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Update Template

Update the template to render the tree:
```html
<div id="app">
    <h1>Multi-Tree List</h1>
    <app-multi-tree-list></app-multi-tree-list>
</div>

<!-- Add CSS or any other styles here -->
<style>
#app {
    margin-top: 60px;
}
</style>
```

### Step 5: Run the Application

Start the development server:
```bash
ng serve
```

Open your browser and navigate to `http://localhost:4200`. The app should now show the multi-tree list based on the provided JSON data.

### Notes

- **Handling Large Data:** For very large datasets, consider using lazy loading techniques or pagination.
- **User Interaction:** Allow users to expand/collapse nodes through click events.
- **Search Functionality:** Optionally add search functionality to filter nodes dynamically.

